1
00:00:01,269 --> 00:00:02,970
Every company has a tooling history.

2
00:00:03,309 --> 00:00:06,630
Maybe you are introducing Cloud Build and Cloud Deploy today,

3
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:10,430
but your organization already has years of

4
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:13,590
investment in Jenkins or GitHub Actions.

5
00:00:13,789 --> 00:00:16,510
They want to ensure the entire pipeline,

6
00:00:16,950 --> 00:00:20,670
no matter the tool, is secure from supply chain attacks.

7
00:00:20,870 --> 00:00:24,920
And the good news is Google Cloud is incredibly flexible,

8
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:29,530
and it integrates seamlessly with third‑party CI tools while

9
00:00:29,530 --> 00:00:32,189
giving you rock solid security foundation.

10
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:36,330
Jenkins is still used heavily in large enterprises,

11
00:00:36,569 --> 00:00:39,529
banks, telecoms, and hybrid environments.

12
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:44,750
Jenkins integrates with Google Cloud through the Jenkins Google OAuth plugin

13
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:48,970
that allows secure authentication with Google identities.

14
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:53,970
Jenkins can trigger Cloud Build to offload heavy builds.

15
00:00:54,169 --> 00:00:57,310
This lets Jenkins orchestrate the workflows,

16
00:00:57,459 --> 00:01:00,620
but lets Cloud Build do the compute heavy work.

17
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:06,870
A very common setup is Jenkins handles the CI and Cloud Deploy handles the dev,

18
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:08,589
staging, and prod rollout.

19
00:01:08,849 --> 00:01:12,902
This provides compliance‑level release, tracking, and rollback

20
00:01:12,902 --> 00:01:16,241
capabilities that Jenkins alone does not offer.

21
00:01:16,491 --> 00:01:20,622
GitHub Actions is now extremely popular in the SaaS

22
00:01:21,111 --> 00:01:23,452
startups and even enterprise teams.

23
00:01:23,641 --> 00:01:27,882
This acts as the CI front end while Google handles the

24
00:01:27,882 --> 00:01:30,052
artifact storage and deployments.

25
00:01:30,241 --> 00:01:33,291
A pretty common pattern is GitHub Actions,

26
00:01:33,372 --> 00:01:36,692
Cloud Build, Artifact Registry, and Cloud Deploy.

27
00:01:37,012 --> 00:01:42,454
Companies adopt this hybrid approach because developers stay in the GitHub,

28
00:01:42,653 --> 00:01:46,790
identities stay secure using the Workload Identity Federation,

29
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:49,054
and builds run on Google Cloud.

30
00:01:49,253 --> 00:01:51,814
This model is now extremely common.

31
00:01:52,134 --> 00:01:54,774
Argo CD is the king of GitOps,

32
00:01:54,893 --> 00:01:59,814
and it pairs beautifully with the Google Kubernetes Engine and Config Connector.

33
00:02:00,054 --> 00:02:03,094
The typical architecture is Cloud Build handles the CI,

34
00:02:03,244 --> 00:02:08,945
Artifact Registry store the images, GitOps repos store the Kubernetes manifest,

35
00:02:09,015 --> 00:02:13,136
and Argo CD automatically syncs to GKE clusters.

36
00:02:13,335 --> 00:02:18,175
The GitOps works very well in the GCP because GKE has a

37
00:02:18,175 --> 00:02:20,955
strong RBAC and native workload identity,

38
00:02:21,175 --> 00:02:26,065
and Config Connector lets GitHub manage both apps and infra.

39
00:02:26,416 --> 00:02:29,115
The most important part is securing the pipeline.

40
00:02:29,345 --> 00:02:34,598
Google Cloud follows SLSA, an industry standard for supply chain security.

41
00:02:34,777 --> 00:02:38,177
It has artifactory registry scanning that every pushed

42
00:02:38,177 --> 00:02:41,768
image is scanned for OS vulnerabilities,

43
00:02:41,988 --> 00:02:44,968
language‑level CVs, and base image issues.

44
00:02:45,138 --> 00:02:48,098
You can block images with critical vulnerabilities.

45
00:02:48,257 --> 00:02:53,658
It has the binary authorization that can enforce only signed images can deploy,

46
00:02:53,938 --> 00:02:56,218
build, and only approved releases.

47
00:02:56,388 --> 00:02:59,770
This prevents tampered containers, compromised build

48
00:02:59,770 --> 00:03:02,070
systems, and unauthorized deployments.

49
00:03:02,210 --> 00:03:06,339
Workload Identity Federation is recommended over service account keys.

50
00:03:06,539 --> 00:03:08,600
Your GitHub Actions, Jenkins,

51
00:03:08,610 --> 00:03:13,779
and Argo CD can all authenticate to Google Cloud without JSON keys,

52
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:16,210
removing a massive security risk.

53
00:03:16,419 --> 00:03:16,660
Also,

54
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:21,539
the tools like OPA Gatekeeper can enforce policy as a code

55
00:03:21,539 --> 00:03:24,929
where you can enforce no privileged containers,

56
00:03:25,289 --> 00:03:28,932
no public buckets, all deployment must declare limits.

57
00:03:29,102 --> 00:03:29,701
And lastly,

58
00:03:29,981 --> 00:03:35,382
CI/CD isolation never runs CI and production workload on the same cluster.

59
00:03:35,542 --> 00:03:40,261
Google Cloud Deploy and Artifact Registry enforce this separation.

60
00:03:40,501 --> 00:03:43,371
So that's how the third‑party tools Jenkins,

61
00:03:43,501 --> 00:03:48,201
GitHub Actions, Argo CD integrate seamlessly with Google Cloud,

62
00:03:48,251 --> 00:03:52,462
and that's how you secure the entire CI/CD supply chain.
